learn:[
{icon:'\ud83d\udcd6',title:'Strategies for Reading Comprehension',text:'Good reading comprehension involves strategies like previewing the text, identifying the main idea and making inferences.',example:'Skimming through headings before reading in detail helps you understand what a passage will be about.',dyk:'Skilled readers often reread difficult sentences or paragraphs multiple times to fully understand challenging texts.',real:'Previewing chapter headings in a textbook before reading in detail is a practical comprehension strategy you likely already use.'},
{icon:'\ud83d\udca1',title:'Identifying the Main Idea',text:'The main idea is the central point or message of a passage, often summarised in a single sentence.',example:'A passage about recycling might have the main idea that reducing waste helps protect the environment.',dyk:'The main idea is sometimes stated directly, often in the first or last sentence, but at other times must be inferred.',real:"Summarising a movie or book you've read in one sentence practises the same skill as finding a passage's main idea."},
{icon:'\ud83d\udd0d',title:'Making Inferences',text:'Making an inference means using clues from the text, combined with your own knowledge, to understand something not directly stated.',example:"If a character 'grabbed an umbrella and rushed outside', we can infer it was raining, even if not directly stated.",dyk:"Making inferences is a skill used constantly in daily life, not just in reading, like guessing someone's mood from their expression.",real:"Understanding a friend's hint without them saying it directly uses the same inference skill as reading comprehension."},
{icon:'\ud83d\udd11',title:'Understanding Context Clues',text:'Context clues are hints within a sentence or passage that help you understand the meaning of an unfamiliar word.',example:"In 'The arid desert had no rainfall for months', context clues suggest 'arid' means very dry.",dyk:"Using context clues effectively can help you understand a text even when you don't know every single word in it.",real:'Figuring out a new word\u2019s meaning from surrounding sentences, without a dictionary, is a skill you use constantly while reading.'},
{icon:'\u270f\ufe0f',title:'Answering Comprehension Questions Effectively',text:'Effective comprehension answers should be based directly on the text, using specific details and evidence from the passage.',example:'When asked why a character felt sad, a good answer points to specific details in the text supporting that feeling.',dyk:'Re-reading the relevant part of a passage before answering a question significantly improves comprehension accuracy.',real:'Going back to check a specific paragraph before answering a question is a smart, effective test-taking strategy.'}
],
revision:{terms:[['Main idea','The central point or message of a passage'],['Inference','Using clues and knowledge to understand something not directly stated'],['Context clue','A hint helping understand an unfamiliar word'],['Skimming','Quickly reading to get a general idea of a text'],['Evidence','Specific details from the text supporting an answer']],
facts:["Skimming headings helps preview a text.","Inferences use clues plus your own knowledge.","Context clues help understand unfamiliar words."]},
summary:'Good reading comprehension uses strategies like previewing text, identifying the main idea, and making inferences using clues from the passage. Context clues help understand unfamiliar words without a dictionary. When answering comprehension questions, effective answers use specific details and evidence directly from the text, and re-reading relevant sections improves accuracy.'
